paragraphs.text:
The original book describes the eventual rest by saying that "the oscillations stop after the reserve of mechanical energy is exhausted."
This does not guarantee that the amplitude becomes exactly zero at some finite instant;
in the usual ideal model of linear viscous damping, the amplitude can keep approaching zero.

More damping is not always better: with excessive damping, the pointer may approach equilibrium slowly, even though it does not necessarily oscillate back and forth.
The electromagnetic damping device described in the original book has a metal plate fixed to the pointer, between the magnetic poles, so that the plate moves with the pointer.
With the magnetic-field arrangement in this device, the motion induces currents in the plate;

The piston's motion forces the liquid through special oil passages. The flow creates resistance, and mechanical energy is dissipated.
This resistance makes the oscillations die down more quickly and helps stabilize the car body.
Here, "stopping quickly" is an ordinary description of the damping effect, not a specification of an exact finite stopping time;
